WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.791
SANDEEP DINESH: If
you're like most people,

00:00:01.791 --> 00:00:03.450
chances are you're
using sources that

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.650
live outside of your cluster.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:08.580
For example, maybe you're
using the Taleo API

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.850
to send text messages, or maybe
the Google Cloud Vision API

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.050
to do image analysis.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.200
If you use the same endpoint
in all of your environments

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.870
and have no plans to bring the
servers into your Kubernetes

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:23.230
cluster, it's perfectly fine
to use the service endpoint

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.300
directly in your code.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.430
However, there
are many scenarios

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.890
where this is not the case.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.630
In this episode of
"Kubernetes Best Practices,"

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.620
let's learn how to leverage
the built-in service discovery

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.060
mechanisms inside Kubernetes for
services outside the cluster,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.640
just like you can for
services inside the cluster.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:54.410
One common service that's a good
example of an external service

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.310
is a database running outside
of your Kubernetes cluster.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:01.460
While some cloud-native
databases, such as Google Cloud

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.650
Data Store or Google
Cloud Spanner,

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.170
use a single endpoint
for all access,

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.180
most databases have
separate endpoints

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.980
for different instances.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.770
However, best practices
for traditional databases,

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:16.010
like MySQL and MongoDB,
usually prescribe

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.170
that you connect to
the different instances

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:19.890
for different environments.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.910
So you might have a big
machine for production data

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:26.070
and maybe a little one
for the test environment.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.740
But each of these will have a
different IP address or domain

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.510
name, and you don't
want to change

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.520
your code from environment
to environment.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.670
So instead of hard-coding
these addresses,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.190
you can actually use
the built-in Kubernetes

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:42.300
DNS-based service discovery
for these external services

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.780
just like you can for
native Kubernetes services.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.240
OK, so let's say you're running
a MongoDB database on Google

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.800
Compute Engine.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.240
So hopefully at
some point you can

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.430
bring a database
inside the cluster,

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.520
but until then, you're
stuck in this hybrid world.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.310
Thankfully, you can use
static Kubernetes services

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.560
to ease some of the pain.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.200
So in this example, I
created a MongoDB server

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.630
using Google Cloud Launcher.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.600
So because it's created in
the same network, or VPC,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.430
as the Kubernetes
cluster, it can

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.040
be accessed using the
high-performance internal IP

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.160
address.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.290
In Google Cloud, this
is the default setup,

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.860
so there's nothing special
you need to configure.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.510
So now we have the IP address.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.300
The first step is
to create a service.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.627
You may notice that there's no
pod selectors for this service.

00:02:29.627 --> 00:02:31.460
So this will create a
service, but it really

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.650
won't know where to
send the traffic.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.540
This allows you
to manually create

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.000
the endpoints object
that'll receive traffic

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.930
from the service.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.790
You can see that the
endpoints manually

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.250
defines the IP address
for the database,

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.680
and it uses the same
name as a service.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.820
Kubernetes will use
all the IP addresses

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.704
to find the endpoints as if they
were regular Kubernetes pods.

00:02:52.704 --> 00:02:54.120
So now you can
access the database

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.190
with a simple connection string.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:59.370
No need to use IP addresses
in your code at all.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.559
If the IP addresses
change in the future,

00:03:01.559 --> 00:03:03.600
you can just update the
endpoints with the new IP

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.890
addresses, and your applications
won't need to make any changes.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.580
If you're using a hosted
database from a third party,

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.177
chances are they give you a
URI that you can connect to.

00:03:15.177 --> 00:03:17.010
If they give you an IP
address, you can just

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.150
use the previous method.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.680
So in this example, I
have two MongoDB databases

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.540
hosted on mLab.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.550
One of them is by dev database,
and the other is production.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.970
The connection strings for
these databases are as follows.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:35.910
Now, mLab gives you a
dynamic URI and dynamic port.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:38.160
And so you can see that
they're both different.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.460
Let's use Kubernetes
to abstract over this.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.590
So let's connect to
the dev database.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.760
You can create an external
name Kubernetes service

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.450
which will give you
a static Kubernetes

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.410
service that redirects traffic
to the external service.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.260
This service will do a
simple CNAME redirect

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.290
at the kernel level so
there's very minimal impact

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.090
on your performance.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.790
So now you can use a much more
simplified connection string.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:07.290
But because external name
uses a CNAME redirection,

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.780
it can't do port remapping.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.870
So this may be OK with
services with static ports,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.330
but unfortunately it falls
short in this example

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.430
where the port is dynamic.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.190
mLab's free tier gives
you a dynamic port number,

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:21.410
and you can't change it.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:23.160
This means you need a
different connection

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.230
string for development
and production,

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:28.480
and you need to hard-code values
for the port, which is bad.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.970
So how can we get port
remapping to work?

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:34.650
The first step is to get
the IP address from the URI.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.920
If you run nslookup,
hostname, or the ping command

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.240
against the URI, you can get
the IP address of the database.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.050
Now you have the IP address.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.350
If the service gives you
back multiple IP addresses,

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:49.810
you can actually use all of
them in the endpoints object.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:52.500
The key thing to remember
is that IP addresses

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.360
behind the URI can
change without notice.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.570
This method is kind of
risky to use in production

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.150
if you aren't sure the
IP address won't change.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.650
With this, you can connect
to the remote database

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.120
without needing to
specify the port.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.160
Kubernetes service transparently
does the port remapping.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.889
So mapping external
sources to internal ones

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:14.930
gives you the flexibility
to bring these services

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.750
into the cluster in the
future while minimizing

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.580
refactoring efforts.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.220
Even if you don't plan on
bringing them in today,

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:24.410
you never know what
tomorrow brings.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:27.200
It also makes it easier
to manage and understand

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.560
what external services
your organization is using.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.410
I'll see you on the next episode
of "Kubernetes Best Practices."

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

